The lantern catches with a soft pop, and warm yellow light spills out — bright for thirty feet and dim for thirty more, throwing the grey shoring timbers into sharp relief. You go to one knee at the threshold. The dirt is a trampled mess: your own boots, the boards prized loose and dragged aside, grit blown in off the slope, and here and there a scuff or a heel-shaped gouge. But three days of wind and passing feet have blurred it all together. If the miners left clear tracks, you cannot separate them from everything else with any certainty. From the tunnel's mouth the cold air keeps moving, smelling of wet stone and old smoke.

Your own hammer is on your belt, as it always is, and your longsword rests beside it. But the lantern already wants a hand, and your shield is strapped to the other arm.

Do you keep the shield and carry the lantern, leaving the hammer sheathed for now — or take a few seconds to doff the shield and go in with lantern and hammer?


